WEBVTT

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:10.840
In the vastness of the Pacific, there's a place unlike any other.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:18.400
Enchanted volcanic islands that are home to a remarkable collection of animals and plants.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:26.720
Here, evolution is proceeding with spectacular speed.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:44.410
that swim in the ocean and spit salt from their noses, thousands of miles from Antarctica,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:59.100
and an abundance of unique plants. Some animals are tiny, and some have only just been discovered.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:22.590
This is a place of wonders, islands born of fire with inhabitants that have transformed

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.340
our understanding of the whole of life on Earth.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.710
lifetime spent making natural history films, I've been to many wonderful places, but none

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:57.000
more extraordinary than here, the Galapagos Islands. These have been called nature's greatest

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
experiment, for here life has changed.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:18.340
has evolved in isolation and produced some extraordinary results.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:24.970
The extraordinary creatures of Galapagos astounded Charles Darwin when he first came here 200

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:30.000
years ago.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.420
to formulate his revelatory theory of evolution by natural selection.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:47.370
And now, two hundred years later, there are still mysteries to be solved and new discoveries to be made.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:59.380
Teams of scientists are investigating unexplored regions of the remoter islands and discovering hitherto unknown animals.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:09.950
on the peaks of its volcanoes, inside networks of immense tunnels within the lava flows,

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:21.290
and in its crystal clear waters. Among a population of giants, and in the magical world

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:30.000
that is revealed by the microscope. New technologies are enabling scientists to investigate

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.860
the workings of evolution in new ways, and producing insights that would have astonished

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:50.780
Darwin himself. Today, we know a lot more about these islands. The discovery of new species,

00:03:50.780 --> 00:04:00.000
long-term studies extending over decades, have given us new perspectives, not just on this place, but on

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.000
the process of evolution worldwide.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:12.770
The islands lie 600 miles from the coast of South America

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:14.760
and straddle the equator.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:23.050
There are 16 of them and a multitude of small islands,

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.740
all separated from the rest of the world

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.730
by the huge expanse of ocean.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:35.570
The biggest island is Isabella.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:43.750
Lying in the center of the group, it has a strange seahorse-like shape.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:50.220
That is because it originated as six separate volcanoes,

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.780
which eventually fused into one great island.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:12.360
The most remote of them is Alcedo, its vast crater is four miles across.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:25.200
The huge, steep-sided walls, still smoking with jets of volcanic gas and steam, make

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.410
this one of the most isolated places in the Galapagos.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:38.970
And it has become a sanctuary for one of the island's most spectacular inhabitants.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.530
Giant tortoises.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.480
There are thousands of them.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:21.880
Are the extraordinary creatures that gave their name to the islands.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:29.830
Galapagos in Spanish means tortoise.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:38.680
And here in the pit of the volcano Alcido, they've assembled in quite some numbers to wallow in the warm volcanic mud.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:56.990
One can weigh as much as a quarter of a ton.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.960
They live for up to 100 years old.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.020
or more which makes them amongst the most long-lived of all vertebrates and being reptiles

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:14.840
they get their energy by basking in the sun but their bodies are so big that once they are warmed

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:30.000
up they can carry on browsing for quite a long time. Existence of creatures like these

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:40.810
so far from the nearest continent, poses many questions. How, for example, did these enormous

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:52.460
beasts get to the islands in the first place? But perhaps the most extraordinary thing about

00:07:52.460 --> 00:08:00.000
the Galapagos tortoises is that they're not all the same. Different islands, different

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:12.310
have different kinds in the heyday there were 15 species they seem to have appeared in an

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:27.300
evolutionary blink of the eye in this tiny cluster of islands and the tortoises are not alone in this

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:36.110
Almost every animal and plant in the islands has a similar story.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:46.560
Animal colonists began to change from the moment they arrived,

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.640
driven to do so by the harsh volcanic landscape around them.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:10.920
There's evidence all around these islands of their direct connection with the furnaces deep in the Earth's crust.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:17.350
But it wasn't until recently that we realized just how close those connections are here.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:26.970
Underneath the section of the Earth's crust on which Galapagos sits, there is something extraordinary.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:40.080
A gigantic column of superheated molten rock rising upwards.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:48.700
This hotspot is immense, at least 60 miles across.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:10:00.000
It extends downwards for 1,800 miles and connects the islands to the very center of the earth.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:11.340
This image, based on the very latest seismological data, shows the hot spot from underneath the crust.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:15.860
This is the source of the island's volcanic activity.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:24.480
It began to build the Galapagos four million years ago.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:29.800
series of islands emerged from the sea. Today there are 16 of them, all of which are volcanoes.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:35.150
Most are now extinct, and the oldest are crumbling into the sea.

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:46.120
But the newer islands are still active and spitting fire.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.970
The youngest is Fernandina.

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:57.460
It rose from the sea just 500,000 years ago.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:09.620
and because it's still active the lava fields that cover it are still unweathered and here in this

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:15.880
desolate barren place we can see how the ingredients of a great natural experiment

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:29.890
came together eight place these islands in a unique spot on this planet they lie plum

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.540
the equator with its year-long warmth and sunshine but perhaps more importantly they also lie at the

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:48.790
crossroads between two competing winds the southeast trade winds blow up from south

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:56.440
america and the northeast trades come down from the caribbean and central america

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.980
These two winds are the lifeblood of the Galapagos.

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:19.050
They carried the earliest settlers to the emerging volcanic islands.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:29.860
Seeds falling from trees in South and Central America were blown across hundreds of miles of oceans.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.140
Most were lost at sea.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:40.730
Of those few that reached the islands, many fell on sterile, baking-hot rocks.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.530
But just a few were luckier.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:49.270
This extraordinary species is related to dandelions.

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:54.600
And it's found a way to grow where there is neither soil nor rain.

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:58.970
A wind-blown seed arrives.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:09.290
drops in a crevice in the lava. Moisture collects and causes it to germinate. With its tiny leaves

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:15.290
it manages to collect more moisture and the infinitesimal quantities of nutrients that

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:23.880
moisture from the skies might contain. Some of these leaves may look dead and indeed they are.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:30.000
The plant is deliberately shedding them. It's ensuring

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:40.290
that nothing it produces is wasted, it's creating its own soil. And eventually, after 80 to 100

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:52.500
years, it produced this, Scalacia, which seems to grow straight out of naked rock. After

00:14:52.500 --> 00:15:00.000
such tiny beginnings, this extraordinary plant has gone from strength to strength. Today,

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:02.710
forests of giant dandelions

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:04.490
blanket the higher slopes of the

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:08.800
island. But some plants

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.060
use a more direct mode of transport

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.030
than a mere gust of wind.

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:19.910
A bird. The albatross

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:21.610
is the king of long

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:24.710
distance flight. It spends

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:26.230
most of its life on the wing.

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:30.000
But each year, it lands

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:45.740
somewhere to breed and raise a chick the appearance of a new island in the middle

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:52.860
of the ocean provided albatrosses with a new nesting site and often these huge

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:59.540
birds brought hitchhikers seeds stuck to their feet

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.890
and in their feathers.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:05.310
They may even have given their hitchhikers

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:16.400
a head start in life with a nice packet of fertilizer.

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:19.660
So gradually, small patches of vegetation

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:25.520
began to appear on the newly emerged islands.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:30.000
The seeds of most trees are too big to be carried far by birds.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.830
or the wind, but those that habitually grow along the coast can use a different form of transport.

00:16:38.860 --> 00:16:50.700
This is the seed of a mangrove, and when it falls, it drops into the sea and floats. This part of it

00:16:50.700 --> 00:17:00.000
is green, so it can make food just like a leaf can, and the seed can remain at sea alive for a

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:02.510
it may float into an estuary.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:06.340
And there the water is brackish and less buoyant,

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:09.150
so the heavy end of the seed falls,

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.960
and it hangs in the water like this.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:17.060
And its tip, it may be at low tide,

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:20.740
trails into the mud and stinks.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:24.170
And the mangrove has planted itself.

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:30.000
These trees are very effective colonies.

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:36.280
mists of newly formed islands.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:43.430
The young seedlings quickly establish themselves.

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:48.750
Their tangled arching roots form a grid which slows down the tidal water searching through

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:54.710
them, causing it to drop its sediment as mud.

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:59.570
At low tide, all kinds of creatures come out to scavenge among the roots.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:18.350
And when the tide is high, other creatures swim in to find shelter.

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:24.130
The waters around the tangle roots serve as nurseries for many species of fish.

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:43.290
So plants created habitats where animals could survive both in water and out of it.

00:18:54.010 --> 00:18:58.020
Some of the very first animals to reach these islands were spiders.

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:09.990
There are some 150 different known species of them in the Galapagos today.

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:20.320
And they travel in a way that is all their own: they balloon.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:26.340
Hatchlings of many species use specially adapted silk.

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:39.600
a spiderling climbs to the tip of a leaf or a twig. There, it produces a thread of silk

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:48.600
from the spinnerets at the end of its abdomen. This 3D electron micrograph shows that this

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:58.200
thread is actually two filaments that are stuck together. It's flattened like a blade.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.000
The slightest wind will catch it.

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:12.160
Once a gust is strong enough, the spiderling lets go with its feet and is carried up and away.

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:30.000
Some can float up to an altitude of several thousand.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:37.780
meters and up there in the trade winds millions of years ago and doubtless many times since

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:51.520
some of them made the 600 mile journey to the galapagos and spiders were not alone

00:20:51.990 --> 00:21:00.000
floating through the skies many different forms of life

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:06.860
brought here by the wind from the South American continent, seeds, pollen, viruses,

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:15.380
bacteria, algae, and insects. Insects, of course, are extremely important in most

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:24.260
ecosystems. They pollinate plants and their food for many other kinds of animals. The species

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:30.000
that have reached here are nearly all the smaller South American species. The bigger ones were

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.940
too heavy to make the journey.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:36.780
But one quite large insect

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:42.670
did so, and its

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:44.870
arrival started a new phase

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:46.930
in the colonisation of the Galapagos.

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:53.370
It was a beetle.

00:21:57.700 --> 00:21:59.090
Beetles are nature's

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:00.000
great recycling.

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:06.130
They chew up organic matter and that helps to create soil.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:16.160
Beetles have sizeable bodies, but also large wings.

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:24.580
That made it possible for one species to make a wind-assisted passage to the Galapagos.

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:50.080
once here these beetles began to change. Later generations had smaller wings. In fact, some Galapagos

00:22:50.080 --> 00:23:00.000
beetles lost their wings altogether. Those individuals with smaller wings were much more likely to

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:08.620
stay put. That is because the big wings that brought the beetles here can equally well

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:19.180
carry them off again. Insects and plants that were brought together in this very arbitrary way

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:27.970
now began to establish new relationships, but one in particular had a very far-reaching effect.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:37.970
Surprisingly, perhaps, flying insects arrived in the Galapagos, not by air, but by sea.

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:43.920
Inside this piece of wood, there is the nest of a little carpenter bee,

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:48.750
whose ancestors must certainly have arrived here in that way.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:24:00.000
This unimpressive little creature was to be of great help to many of the newly established plants

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:13.850
It fed on their nectar, and pollinated them.

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:25.230
Carpenter bees are still the main pollinators on the islands,

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:27.910
and the plants have adapted accordingly.

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:40.210
Nearly all the flowers on the Galapagos are now either white or yellow.

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:48.120
Those are the colors preferred by the carpenter bees.

00:24:48.740 --> 00:24:51.180
So there's no point in being anything else.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:24:59.980
So land plants...

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:10.810
flourished in the sea there was another factor that helped the colonists

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:19.600
amazingly it came not from the nearest land South America but from 8,000 miles

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:27.800
away across the Pacific in the other direction to the west from the tropical

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:30.000
rainforests of New Guinea

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:56.820
Here, there are heavy downpours every day.

00:25:56.820 --> 00:25:59.570
Washes nutrients from the forest soil,

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:08.400
Down streams, into rivers, and finally into the ocean.

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:15.500
And there, swept up by the currents, they're carried across the Pacific to the Galapagos.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:22.990
They travel not near the surface, but in the depths by a cold water current.

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:26.990
It's one of three that converge on the islands.

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:30.000
Another comes from the Panamera.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:41.930
Basin and yet another originates near Peru. This convergence of currents has had a remarkable

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:56.220
impact on life in the islands. Scientists led by marine biologist Stuart Banks are today

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:58.040
investigating their effect.

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:07.490
Well, Galapagos is unique in the sense that it's a system in the tropics.

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:10.530
It's lying right on the equator under the strong equatorial sun.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:16.030
And these are usually systems which are very considered to be deserts for productivity.

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:19.550
But Galapagos is different. There's a unique confluence of currents,

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:23.740
and most importantly, a submarine undercurrent called the Cromwell current.

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:29.320
And these undercurrents are bringing micronutrients up into these sunlit waters.

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:39.070
The Galapagos Islands in the open Pacific lie in the path of these converging currents.

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:46.920
They deflect the cold, nutrient-laden waters upwards to mingle with the warm water above.

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:57.400
This mixing creates ideal conditions for a vast community of floating microscopic plants: phytoplankton.

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:17.440
organism is only a few microns across and invisible to the naked eye yet these

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:26.160
specs of life underpin the whole Galapagos ecosystem and here the

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:30.000
fertilizer from New Guinea enables them to hugely increase

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.070
increase in both variety and number.

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:47.660
Scientists have now discovered that the islands themselves provide the phytoplankton with

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:58.090
something that is crucial for its growth, a vital life-enhancing element, iron.

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:00.000
Now the undercurrent which hits the wet

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:06.180
side of the archipelago it's a bit like imagining pointing a hose against the side of a wall it

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:12.370
forms filaments that physically that physically kind of spread around the archipelago and up

00:29:12.370 --> 00:29:19.430
into the surface and it's thought that it's the abrasion and the leaching against the volcanic

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:26.060
platform of the islands which is bringing iron up into the surface waters so thanks to that

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:30.000
unique situation you tend to get these huge phytoplankton blues

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.080
and this is literally you're talking about

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:35.830
literally millions of these tiny organisms coming together.

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:43.990
This extraordinary image based on satellite data

00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:49.530
shows how blooms of phytoplankton grow and shrink over the seasons.

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:00.000
These astronomic numbers of microscopic plants

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:18.960
support another vast community: microscopic animals, zooplankton. Here, under the waves,

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:30.000
there is a living world of extraordinary complexity and beauty. All these tiny creatures

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:39.840
are dependent on the rich blooms of the phytoplankton.

00:30:39.840 --> 00:31:00.000
Some graze on them, others graze on the grazers, many equally

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:02.990
Extraordinary creatures feed on the rich soup.

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:18.650
From small crustaceans and juvenile jellyfish to the young of many fish.

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:37.720
These tiny animals and plants in turn support shoals of larger fish

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:42.420
that swarm in such numbers and variety that they make the Galapagos waters

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:47.670
among the most diverse of all marine ecosystems.

00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:05.660
Many extraordinary creatures feed directly on the plankton itself.

00:32:07.720 --> 00:32:12.900
Garden eels are quite small, some 15 centimetres or so long.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:30.000
Bigger fish also feed on the plankton.

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:54.640
turn are food for hunters. Among them, the Galapagos shark, a relative of the tiger shark.

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:11.970
and scalloped hammerhead sharks, which today congregate here in numbers that are unequaled

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:28.830
anywhere else in the world.

00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:30.000
The schools of female.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:34.160
are often surrounded by an outer ring of patrolling males.

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:37.900
No one is quite sure what's happening at these times.

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:42.950
It's probably part of their mating behavior.

00:33:42.950 --> 00:33:43.670
Music

00:34:05.920 --> 00:34:10.660
Many coastal species are unique to these islands.

00:34:10.660 --> 00:34:19.990
This is the red-lipped batfish.

00:34:19.990 --> 00:34:30.000
Its lower fins have been modified to enable it to prowl across the sea floor.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:40.710
A sea robin can also walk and flashes its bright pectoral fins to frighten away predators.

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:46.610
The trumpetfish has such an elongated body that it's hard to see, so it's able to sneak

00:34:46.610 --> 00:34:49.080
up on its prey.

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:16.740
there are giants here too. This is the Mola Mola, the Sunfish. It's huge, three meters

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:30.000
across and addicted to lying on its side at the surface. It eats vast quantities of jellyfish. And there are not only fish

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:38.030
swimming in these waters, there are mammals, sea lions, whose ancestors originally came

00:35:38.030 --> 00:35:54.260
from the coasts of California. The Galapagos plankton is so abundant, it attracts some

00:35:54.260 --> 00:36:00.000
of the biggest of all ocean mammals: humpback whales.

00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:19.570
rivaling them in size, the biggest of all fish, 20-ton whale shark.

00:36:25.630 --> 00:36:30.000
Few parts of the world's oceans can equal these Galapagos waters.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.540
for sheer variety and abundance of marine life.

00:36:37.100 --> 00:36:40.160
And this richness in turn

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:43.110
has attracted a great variety of seabirds.

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:52.820
Many are long-distance travellers.

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:00.000
The islands have become the best place in highlands

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.790
hundreds of square miles of open ocean, for many birds to rest and to breed.

00:37:07.610 --> 00:37:10.590
The Nazca boobies range across the whole of the Pacific,

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:15.280
but this waved albatross lives nowhere else but here.

00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:23.480
The male frigate bird has a pouch of scarlet skin hanging from his neck.

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:30.000
During the breeding season, he inflates it to attract a mate or see off a ride.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:55.810
there's also another kind of booby the blue-footed his spectacular feet are the

00:37:55.810 --> 00:38:00.000
key elements in his courtship display in which he tries to persuade

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:07.720
his mate that his really are the bluest feet around.

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:32.740
are superb

00:38:32.740 --> 00:38:33.400
fishermen.

00:38:36.390 --> 00:38:45.010
Once they spot a shoal,

00:38:45.250 --> 00:38:47.310
they fly up to a height of 25

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:49.440
meters, and then

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.290
they dive into the water

00:38:51.290 --> 00:38:53.590
at speeds of 60 miles an hour

00:38:53.590 --> 00:39:00.000
or more. Water with such

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:10.850
force could kill many birds but boobies have special air sacs in their heads that cushion the impact

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:44.020
Cormorants are coastal birds rather than ocean travelers, so they can only have arrived here

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:50.830
by accident, having probably been swept out to sea by a gale. But they arrived a very long

00:39:50.830 --> 00:40:00.000
time ago, and they stayed. Like cormorants worldwide, the Galapagos species is a superb

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:17.230
swimmer. Its legs are powerful paddles and the body itself is beautifully

00:40:17.230 --> 00:40:30.000
streamlined. In effect, the cormorant flies under

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:55.350
water and it's certainly able to outmaneuver many a fish. The Galapagos

00:40:55.350 --> 00:41:00.000
coast is a great place for a cormorant. There are plenty of

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:08.930
of excellent nesting sites and there are no land predators that might threaten a bird

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:17.460
sitting in such a vulnerable place its ancestors when they first arrived had wings like any other

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:27.490
cormorant but with no need to fly its wings over generations became smaller and smaller

00:41:36.170 --> 00:41:43.390
Now they are mere stumps with a few tattered feathers.

00:41:43.390 --> 00:41:49.040
So now the bird can't fly, even if it wanted to.

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:50.840
And since it's flightless,

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:54.200
there is no disadvantage in growing bigger.

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:56.770
And the Galapagos cormorant is now heavier

00:41:56.770 --> 00:41:58.930
than any of its flying relatives.

00:42:06.560 --> 00:42:11.210
With nothing to hassle it and plenty of fish in the sea alongside, the cormorants can now

00:42:11.210 --> 00:42:18.900
concentrate on caring for their young, and in fact some manage to raise three broods

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:20.040
each season.

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:36.060
But there is another permanent resident here whose history is even more remarkable.

00:42:40.700 --> 00:42:45.660
Its ancestors lived 5,000 miles away in the Antarctic.

00:42:49.280 --> 00:42:54.080
The creature was a penguin.

00:42:57.090 --> 00:42:59.190
Penguins are ocean-going swimmers.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.840
But a few thousand years ago, some of them got caught in the cold waters of the Humboldt

00:43:04.840 --> 00:43:10.980
Current and were carried northwards up the coast of South America and out to the Galapagos.

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:18.570
They could hardly have found anywhere more different from their polar home.

00:43:18.920 --> 00:43:25.200
And in response they change, the emperor penguin that lives near the South Pole stands over

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:30.000
a meter high, Galapagos.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:49.490
penguin is now only half as tall. And that helps a lot in the Galapagos. Small animals

00:43:49.490 --> 00:43:52.180
lose heat much faster than big ones.

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:06.660
and the penguins have developed behavioral tricks as well.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:16.480
Their feet are easily sunburnt,

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:19.400
so they do their best to keep them covered.

00:44:31.820 --> 00:44:35.540
And some parts of the sea around the islands are quite cool.

00:44:35.540 --> 00:44:40.160
The Humboldt current flowing up from the Antarctic

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:43.370
and washing around the western parts of the archipelago

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:45.040
is still quite chilly.

00:44:46.340 --> 00:44:50.200
So most of the penguins stay in the channel

00:44:50.200 --> 00:44:53.490
between the two westernmost islands.

00:45:02.240 --> 00:45:04.660
And when things get really hot,

00:45:04.660 --> 00:45:13.840
they can still cool off with the swim.

00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:16.260
They're quick to detect the slightest variation

00:45:16.260 --> 00:45:19.460
in temperature and move around to find places

00:45:19.460 --> 00:45:22.370
where an eddy might have water-pleasing chill.

00:45:34.440 --> 00:45:39.600
the arrival of penguins must be the most unlikely event in the whole story

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:48.200
of the colonization of the calabacus but the most important and influential animals

00:45:48.200 --> 00:46:00.000
had yet to appear not birds but reptiles many million years ago somewhere in south

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:07.080
or Central America, a reptile, an iguana, was grazing close to the banks of one of the

00:46:07.080 --> 00:46:30.000
great rivers. Perhaps it was feeding on floating vegetation. Maybe it fell onto such a

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:31.390
raft from a tree.

00:46:37.070 --> 00:46:38.790
Patches of floating vegetation

00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:41.050
are still swept out

00:46:41.050 --> 00:46:43.080
into the estuaries by flash

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:45.060
floods or tropical storms.

00:46:48.480 --> 00:46:49.840
Many are quite big

00:46:49.840 --> 00:46:52.550
and easily buoyant enough to support

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:54.200
a metre-long iguana.

00:46:58.600 --> 00:47:00.000
And sometimes they

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:08.360
don't break up but float out to the open ocean who knows how many thousands of

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:14.180
animals of many kinds have been lost at sea on rafts like these dying from thirst

00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:30.000
and exposure reptiles are very tough they can go without food or

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:38.750
water for days, weeks, even months. No mammal can survive such hardships as long as they can.

00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:50.770
And at some point in the history of the Galapagos, the currents carried an iguana

00:47:50.770 --> 00:48:00.000
across 600 miles of ocean to the islands. No doubt it happened not one

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:12.930
Once, but several times, and here the iguanas settled and multiplied.

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:20.590
Today there are thousands of them, so many and so widely distributed throughout the islands

00:48:20.590 --> 00:48:24.740
that they are now one of the Galapagos' most famous inhabitants.

00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:40.960
but these are the most celebrated of all.

00:48:44.340 --> 00:48:46.540
The ones that gave the islands their name.

00:48:47.590 --> 00:48:48.650
Giant tortoises.

00:48:54.050 --> 00:48:57.610
Tortoises can't swim, but they can float.

00:48:58.550 --> 00:49:00.000
And about 3 million...

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:06.080
years ago, one of them, a large species from the South American forests, was

00:49:06.080 --> 00:49:13.510
carried away perhaps by a flash flood and swept out to sea. After weeks, maybe

00:49:13.510 --> 00:49:20.430
even months, they eventually landed on an island. One of them, perhaps a gravid

00:49:20.430 --> 00:49:29.400
female, produced eggs. As time passed, they spread into other islands in the archipelago.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:31.300
giant tortoises

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:32.470
had arrived

00:49:32.470 --> 00:49:34.000
in the Galapagos

00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:43.620
with this

00:49:43.620 --> 00:49:44.920
small selection

00:49:44.920 --> 00:49:45.680
of animals

00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:46.460
and plants

00:49:46.460 --> 00:49:47.240
in place

00:49:47.240 --> 00:49:48.820
nature's great

00:49:48.820 --> 00:49:49.460
experiment

00:49:49.460 --> 00:49:50.760
gathered pace

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:57.430
forged by fire

00:49:57.430 --> 00:50:00.000
fueled by

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:11.160
by the ocean, fanned by the winds, and seeded by a very few and very different species.

00:50:12.980 --> 00:50:19.850
A new community was established here in the Galapagos, and one with a very small but very

00:50:19.850 --> 00:50:21.910
oddly assorted cast of characters.

00:50:23.050 --> 00:50:24.190
There were no amphibians.

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:28.810
Because of their porous skin, they're poisoned by seawater.

00:50:28.810 --> 00:50:30.000
There were no mammals.

00:50:30.060 --> 00:50:37.490
animals except for a small short-tailed rat. Flying insects and seeds of plants had reached

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:45.140
here, bought by the wind. But fundamentally, this was a land of birds which flew here and

00:50:45.140 --> 00:50:53.100
reptiles which floated here. And together, they had to make a living on this bare, rocky

00:50:53.100 --> 00:51:00.000
island that was so crucially different from the well-watered, luxuriant forests from which

00:51:03.870 --> 00:51:10.500
In the next programme, we will discover how this strange, oddly assorted cast of characters

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:15.720
learned to colonise even the most hostile parts of the Galapagos,

00:51:15.720 --> 00:51:22.850
and to live with one another, and how they changed in the process.

00:51:22.850 --> 00:51:29.960
And we venture even deeper into the islands, into places where, even today, new species

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:35.760
are being discovered.
